Here’s a single‑file HTML page that does exactly what you asked for: a button that, when clicked, shows a random joke from a list of four jokes.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      text-align: center;
      margin-top: 5rem;
      background: #f9f9f9;
    }
    #joke {
      margin-top: 1.5rem;
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      color: #333;
      min-height: 2rem;   /* keep space even when empty */
    }
    button {
      padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
      font-size: 1rem;
      cursor: pointer;
      background: #007bff;
      color: #fff;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
    button:hover {
      background: #0056b3;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Click for a Random Joke!</h1>
  <button id="jokeBtn">Tell me a joke</button>
  <div id="joke"></div>

  <script>
    // Array of jokes
    const jokes = [
      "Why don’t scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
      "I told my wife she was drawing her eyebrows too high. She looked surprised.",
      "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
      "I’m reading a book on anti‑gravity. It’s impossible to put down!"
    ];

    // Grab elements
    const btn = document.getElementById('jokeBtn');
    const jokeDiv = document.getElementById('joke');

    // Function to pick a random joke
    function getRandomJoke() {
      const idx = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
      return jokes[idx];
    }

    // Event listener for button click
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      jokeDiv.textContent = getRandomJoke();
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
1. **HTML** – A button (`#jokeBtn`) and a `<div>` (`#joke`) to display the joke.
2. **CSS** – Simple styling to center the content and make the button look nice.
3. **JavaScript** –  
   * An array `jokes` holds the four jokes.  
   * `getRandomJoke()` picks a random index.  
   * Clicking the button triggers the event listener, which updates the `<div>` with a new random joke.

Save the code as `index.html` and open it in any browser. Each click will show a different joke (or the same one again, since it’s random). Enjoy!

**Solution Overview**

The task is to list all values that appear in *both* of two integer arrays, **without allocating any extra data structures** (no hash‑tables, no auxiliary vectors, etc.).  
The only “extra” memory we may use is a few integer variables for indices and counters.

The most efficient way that respects the “no extra data structures” rule is:

1. **Sort** each array *in place* (e.g. `std::sort` in C++ or `Arrays.sort` in Java).  
   Sorting does not require any additional containers – it rearranges the elements inside the original array.

2. **Two‑pointer scan**  
   After sorting, the arrays are ordered. We walk through them simultaneously with two indices `i` and `j`.  
   * If `a[i] == b[j]` we have found a common element – print it and advance both indices.  
   * If `a[i] < b[j]` we advance `i` (the smaller value cannot match any later element of `b`).  
   * Otherwise advance `j`.

The algorithm runs in  
`O(n log n + m log m)` time (dominated by the two sorts) and uses only `O(1)` extra space.

